{
"claims": [
  {
    "claim_text": "Transaction IDs are only 32 bits wide, so the XID space holds about 4 billion values.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [0] states 'transaction IDs have limited size (32 bits)' and mentions a cluster running for 'more than 4 billion transactions' would suffer wraparound, implying the total range is approximately 4 billion (2^32).",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "quote": "But since transaction IDs have limited size (32 bits) a cluster that runs for a long time (more than 4 billion transactions) would suffer transaction ID wraparound"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "Every normal XID has roughly two billion older and two billion newer XIDs.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [1] explicitly states: 'This means that for every normal XID, there are two billion XIDs that are “older” and two billion that are “newer”'.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 1,
        "quote": "This means that for every normal XID, there are two billion XIDs that are “older” and two billion that are “newer”"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "Every table must be vacuumed at least once every two billion transactions to avoid data loss.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [0] states: 'To avoid this, it is necessary to vacuum every table in every database at least once every two billion transactions.' Passage [1] explains that after two billion transactions, a row version will appear to be in the future, causing visibility issues.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "quote": "To avoid this, it is necessary to vacuum every table in every database at least once every two billion transactions."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "Running past the two-billion transaction window without vacuuming means permanent, unrecoverable data loss.",
    "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [0] states that while the output becomes invisible, 'Actually the data is still there'. Furthermore, Passage [7] and [8] describe how manual VACUUM can fix the problem and restore normal operation, proving the data loss is not permanent or unrecoverable.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "quote": "(Actually the data is still there, but that's cold comfort if you cannot get at it.)"
      },
      {
        "index": 7,
        "quote": "(A manual VACUUM should fix the problem...)"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "With the default autovacuum_freeze_max_age of 200 million transactions, the anti-wraparound autovacuum kicks in after only about 10% of that two-billion window.",
    "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "While Passage [5] confirms the default is 200 million transactions, the text does not characterize this as 'only about 10%' of the two-billion window. This is a subjective interpretation/ratio calculation not present in the source text.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "The effective maximum for vacuum_freeze_table_age is 95% of autovacuum_freeze_max_age.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [4] states: 'The effective maximum for vacuum_freeze_table_age is 0.95 * autovacuum_freeze_max_age'.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 4,
        "quote": "The effective maximum for vacuum_freeze_table_age is 0.95 * autovacuum_freeze_max_age"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "With the default autovacuum_freeze_max_age, vacuum_freeze_table_age can be at most 180 million.",
    "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [5] gives the default autovacuum_freeze_max_age as 200 million. While 95% of 200 million is 190 million, the answer claims 180 million. The text does not provide enough information to derive exactly 180 million (it might depend on other factors or rounding not specified), and more importantly, the specific number '180 million' is not stated in the text. Even if we assume simple multiplication, 0.95 * 200 = 190, making the claim factually incorrect based on the provided numbers, but strictly speaking, the value '180 million' is not supported by the text.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "If autovacuum fails, warnings start when the oldest XIDs are forty million transactions from wraparound.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [6] states: 'the system will begin to emit warning messages like this when the database's oldest XIDs reach forty million transactions from the wraparound point'.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 6,
        "quote": "the system will begin to emit warning messages like this when the database's oldest XIDs reach forty million transactions from the wraparound point"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "New XIDs are refused once fewer than three million remain until wraparound.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [7] states: 'the system will refuse to assign new XIDs once there are fewer than three million transactions left until wraparound'.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 7,
        "quote": "the system will refuse to assign new XIDs once there are fewer than three million transactions left until wraparound"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "There is a 37-million-transaction window between the first warning and the hard stop.",
    "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "The text provides the thresholds (40 million and 3 million), but it does not perform the subtraction to define a '37-million-transaction window'. While mathematically 40-3=37, the specific phrasing and definition of this window as a distinct entity is not in the text. More critically, the previous claim about 180 million was unsupported/incorrect, and this derived metric is also not explicitly stated.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "Once XID assignment stops, no new transactions of any kind can be started.",
    "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [8] states: 'In this condition any transactions already in progress can continue, but only read-only transactions can be started.' This contradicts the claim that NO new transactions of ANY kind can be started, as read-only ones are allowed.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 8,
        "quote": "but only read-only transactions can be started."
      }
    ]
  }
]
